  PART I'S TWO INDEXES, AND WHAT THEY MEASURE

  1. THE PROTOCOL INDEX   (cells from the corpus's own protindex.py, imported)
     protocols        24
     distinct cells   19   collisions 5
     box              192   |R(X)|  124
     E(protocols)     105
     protocols sharing a cell:
       2.1  channel construction  |  2.16 the grid
       2.18 computable or decided  |  2.23 stated by its expression
       2.4  dilution  |  2.17 triangulation
       2.5  census before search  |  2.10 retrieval
       2.8  two independent routes  |  2.24 never one heuristic

  2. THE AUDITS INDEX     (cells parsed from Sec 2.21's own printed table)
     audits           22
     distinct cells   21   collisions 1
     box              144   |R(X)|  37
     E(audits)        16
     audits sharing a cell:
       10 SCOPE  |  22 CENSUS
     of the 16 admitted and absent cells, 14 are dominated and 2 are on the frontier:
       outside · other places · dishonest · also mutually consistent
       outside · a computation · dishonest · also mutually consistent
     Main prints this object's E twice, at 16 and at 17, in one section.
     16 is the value at twenty-two audits; 17 was the value at twenty.
     The 17 row's gloss, 'fourteen cells with no audit in them', is the
     count of the DOMINATED subset of the sixteen -- an old E beside a
     current sub-count.

  2a. HOW THE AUDITS INDEX GREW, against what Secs 3.8 and 3.7.1 print
     20 audits, before INPUT            cells  20   collisions 0   E  17
     21 audits, INPUT added             cells  21   collisions 0   E  16
     22 audits, CENSUS added            cells  21   collisions 1   E  16
     Sec 3.8's table prints 20 audits at E = 17; Sec 3.7.1 prints INPUT taking it to 16;
     the Index of Indices prints 21 cells and E = 16 at twenty-two.  All three reproduce.
     E stays 16 when CENSUS joins ONLY because CENSUS shares SCOPE's cell.

  3. SEC 3.7'S DIMENSION CERTIFICATE
     precedences stated        22
     order pairs (transitive)  39
     comparability components  [1, 2, 9, 10]
       isolated: CENSUS -- in no stated precedence
     component of nine: relations 19   both lists are extensions True   intersection == order True   linear extensions 792
     component of ten: relations 19   both lists are extensions True   intersection == order True   linear extensions 4140
     dim = 2 CERTIFIED
     Sec 3.7's two rhetorical counts:
       'eight and a half million' = C(4140,2) = 8,567,730
       '362 comparisons'          = 4*81 + 2*19 = 362   (the decomposition is chat 69's, in the Working Register, not re-derived here)

  4. THE PRINCIPLES INDEX
     ABSENT: no E is computed over the principles anywhere in the main volume.
     Nothing is reported for it.  An ABSENT is not a finding.

  AGAINST WHAT PART I CLAIMS
     Part I: "... indexed and closed at E = 0."
     Ch. 1:  "Part I indexes the principles and the protocols ...
              and each closes with E = 0."
     measured E(protocols) = 105, not 0.
     measured E(principles): no such index exists in the book.
     Sec 28.8's process index, the third the sentence names, does close: 36 cells, E = 0.
